A select's case variable is bound by symbol, not by the name it was written as.

fyne's tick arm names its parameter `at`, and a case variable Go considers
unused is a compile error -- so the emitter binds one only where the arm's body
reads it. Asking that by *name* asks the wrong question: a program with state of
its own spelled `at` emits `m.at` in the tick body, which is not the parameter
and does not read it, but did make the emitter write `case at := <-ticker.C:`.
Go then rejected the file with `declared and not used: at`, for a program whose
only crime was picking a variable name the platform package had also picked.

The arm below reads nothing of the tick's own payload, so it binds nothing --
and the program's `at` is the model field it always was.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `case at :=` -- the arm's body reads the program's own `at`, never the parameter, so binding one leaves Go with a variable nothing uses

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

ui.window(title="t") {
    var at = 0

    time.timer(interval=1000ms, enabled=true, @tick { at += 1 })
    ui.text(value=string(at))
}
